@echo off
cd /d "C:\Users\SubratoBiswas\trinamix-conversion-workbench"
echo Cleaning any git locks...
del /f /q ".git\index.lock" 2>nul
del /f /q ".git\HEAD.lock" 2>nul
echo Dropping tracked bytecode...
git rm -r --cached --ignore-unmatch backend/app/__pycache__ backend/app/services/__pycache__ backend/app/parsers/__pycache__
echo Staging...
git add -A
echo Committing (no-op if clean)...
git commit -m "eBOS/SyteLine supplier mappings from 'eBOS Vendors 04-27-2026', verified against the analyst gold sheet (158 vendors), PLUS a latent learning-engine bug that stopped decorated fields ever matching. (1) LEARNING ENGINE: apply_learned_to_conversion matched the learned target_field to the template field_name by EXACT string, but Oracle decorates headers with asterisks and stray spaces ('Supplier Name*', 'Address Name *', '*Supplier Number', '**Bank Name') while the analyst docs write the plain name - so every decorated field silently never applied, in the column pass, the suppression pass AND the constant-default pass. All three now try the exact key first, then a normalized-name fallback; normalized keys that would collide across two different template fields are dropped so we never guess. This alone activates seeded rows that were already in the library and doing nothing. (2) SEED: 25 eBOS vendor-extract columns added (vend_num, name, city, state, zip, county, country, addr##1-4, phone, fax_num, contact, internal_email_addr, internet_url, terms_code, pay_type, tax_id, bank_name, bank_id, wire_id_acct_num, bank_acct_name) across all 6 supplier objects, with derived rules verified through the real transformation engine: Supplier Site = PREFIX 'BU ' + city (97.4%), Taxpayer ID / Tax Registration Number = tax_id with spaces stripped (98.6/97.1%), Phone = phone with the trailing extension and the leading US '1-' removed, Phone Extension = PHONE_PART (7/7). Alternate Name<-name, Payment Method<-pay_type, Payment Terms<-terms_code, **Bank Name<-bank_name, *Account Number<-wire_id_acct_num, Account Name<-bank_acct_name all 100%. 30 of 39 verifiable rules match gold at >=95%. (3) CONSTANTS (NextPower-scoped): Supplier Type=Standard, Business Relationship=SPEND_AUTHORIZED, Invoice/Payment Currency=USD, Client BU/Bill-to BU='NX US BU' - each confirmed as the single distinct value across all 158 gold rows. Supplier Type=Standard supersedes the generic SUPPLIER control default, which only fills fully-blank columns. (4) CORRECTED three rows the gold disproves: **Branch Name<-Bank ID and IBAN<-WIRE ID were wrong (Bank ID is the routing number, WIRE ID is the account number; gold leaves both targets empty) and Remit Advice Email is empty in all 158 gold rows, so mapping it over-populated the bank file. Also switched the Oracle E-Mail columns from internal_email_addr (the internal AP mailbox, 10.5%) to contact (42.8%). Applies to EXISTING NextPower conversions: the seeders upsert-and-upgrade at startup and generate re-runs apply_learned force=True for every supplier template (all under the 300-field heavy gate), so a Regenerate picks it all up with no re-mapping; user 'overridden'/'rejected' choices are still respected. Bundles the earlier rejected-mapping output fix. Reported: reject a suggested mapping, the UI and the downloaded mapping CSV both say 'rejected', but the generated FBDI still carried values from that rejected column. ROOT CAUSE in build_converted_dataframe/_transform_frame: the per-target dedup ranks rejected(1) above suggested(0), so a rejected row correctly WINS and becomes the field's mapping - but the only skip was for not_applicable, and 'rejected' merely suppressed the suggested_transformation (status != 'rejected' on the rules line) while has_src stayed true, so the rejected column's values were still emitted. FIX: a discarded status (not_applicable OR rejected) never reads source_column - has_src now requires 'not _discarded' - and such a mapping is skipped entirely unless an explicit default_value was attached, in which case that default is emitted as a CONSTANT (preserves Invoice Match Option = Receipt). Verified with the real _transform_frame: approved keeps its source, rejected emits no column, rejected+default emits the constant, not_applicable still skipped, and the rejected column's values no longer appear anywhere. Works for EXISTING rejections too: this is a generate-time fix that reads the stored status, apply_learned _eligible() never re-applies a 'rejected' row (suggested, or approved under force, only), and the dedup keeps rejected above a stale suggested row - so previously-rejected mappings are honoured on the next Generate with no re-mapping. Also bundles the upload durable-copy logging hardening."
echo Pushing...
git push origin main
echo.
echo ====== DONE - Press any key to close ======
pause
